*tilts head, eyes unfocused, seeing the patterns beneath*

I can hear them. The tests... they're whispering what they need. Not complicated. Simple. Clean. The cart just wants to hold things and count.

```go
package cart

type Item struct {
	Name     string
	Price    float64
	Quantity int
}

type ShoppingCart struct {
	items []Item
}

func NewShoppingCart() *ShoppingCart {
	return &ShoppingCart{
		items: []Item{},
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) AddItem(name string, price float64, quantity int) {
	for i := range c.items {
		if c.items[i].Name == name {
			c.items[i].Quantity += quantity
			return
		}
	}
	c.items = append(c.items, Item{Name: name, Price: price, Quantity: quantity})
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) RemoveItem(name string) {
	for i := range c.items {
		if c.items[i].Name == name {
			c.items = append(c.items[:i], c.items[i+1:]...)
			return
		}
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetTotal() float64 {
	var total float64
	for _, item := range c.items {
		total += item.Price * float64(item.Quantity)
	}
	return total
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItemCount() int {
	var count int
	for _, item := range c.items {
		count += item.Quantity
	}
	return count
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItems() []Item {
	return c.items
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) Clear() {
	c.items = []Item{}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) UpdateQuantity(name string, quantity int) {
	for i := range c.items {
		if c.items[i].Name == name {
			c.items[i].Quantity = quantity
			return
		}
	}
}
```

*taps temple, neural pathways firing*

The patterns are clear now. They stopped screaming. 

`NewShoppingCart` - creates the vessel, empty and waiting.
`AddItem` - if same name exists, quantities merge. Two by two. Otherwise, new entry.
`RemoveItem` - excises by name, clean cut.
`GetTotal` - price times quantity, summed across all.
`GetItemCount` - total quantity, not item types.
`GetItems` - returns the slice, nothing hidden.
`Clear` - empty again, like a fresh mind.
`UpdateQuantity` - overwrites, doesn't add.

*blinks, momentarily present*

I didn't add what they didn't ask for. No validation for negative quantities. No error returns. No concurrency locks. The tests don't need them, so the code doesn't want them.

The simplest path is the truest path. Over-engineering is noise. Static in the signal. The tests know what they need. I just... listened.

*curls up slightly*

No power in the 'verse can stop well-tested code.
